## Architecture — Tiered Layers

[REPLACE — describe your application's layered architecture. The template below follows IDesign principles. Adjust layer names and rules to match your architecture.]

All components follow tiered layering. **No layer skipping.**

```
Client → Manager → Engine → Data Access → Resource (external)
                          ↘ Utility (cross-cutting)
         Infrastructure (DI, composition root)
         Contracts (interfaces, types — leaf)
```

| Layer | Directory | Responsibility | Rules |
|-------|-----------|---------------|-------|
| **Client** | (UI project) | UI, user interaction | Presentation only. No business logic. |
| **Manager** | `manager/` | Orchestration, workflow | Stateless mediators. No business logic. Delegates to engines. |
| **Engine** | `engine/` | Business logic | Pure functions: `(data, rules) → results`. No I/O. No DA calls. |
| **Data Access** | `data_access/` | Data retrieval/persistence | Database queries, API calls, file I/O. No business logic. |
| **Utility** | `utility/` | Cross-cutting services | Logging, audit, sync, notifications. Domain-adjacent, not business logic. |
| **Infrastructure** | `infrastructure/` | Framework plumbing | DI container, composition roots, provisional mocks. |
| **Contracts** | `contracts/` | Interfaces & shared types | Protocols, DTOs, error hierarchies. Zero intra-project imports. |
| **Resource** | (external) | External systems | Databases, APIs, file systems. Not code we write — what DA adapts to. |

### Import Direction (top → bottom only)

```
manager/        → contracts/, infrastructure/  (resolves deps via DI)
engine/         → contracts/                   (receives data, returns results)
data_access/    → contracts/, utility/         (implements DA interfaces, may use helpers)
utility/        → contracts/                   (implements utility Protocols)
infrastructure/ → contracts/                   (except composition root, which wires all layers)
contracts/      → (leaf — zero intra-project imports, only stdlib/third-party)
```

**Forbidden:** `engine/` → `data_access/`, `data_access/` → `engine/`, `engine/` → `manager/`, any layer → `manager/` (except Client). `contracts/` → anything.

## Agent Coordination

Agents coordinate through **files in the repo**, not shared memory:

| Artifact | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `CLAUDE.md` | Shared project context (auto-loaded every session) |
| `Design/` docs | Architect → Tester → Implementer handoff |
| `Design/exec-plans/` | Task tracking between sessions |
| `.claude/rules/` | Path-specific coding standards (auto-loaded) |
| Git commits | Status checkpoints between phases |

### Workflow: PR-Driven (Ralph Wiggum Loop)
1. Human or architect agent creates exec-plan in `exec-plans/active/`
2. Tester agent writes failing tests (test-first)
3. Implementer agent (works in isolated worktree) writes code to make tests pass
4. Implementer agent self-reviews (quality gate: build, lint, tests)
5. Implementer agent opens MR/PR via CLI
6. Hook auto-triggers → reviewer agent reviews diff, posts severity-tagged findings
7. If Critical findings → implementer auto-fixes → pushes → re-review (loop)
8. When zero Critical findings → reviewer approves → human final approval

### Orchestrator Delegation Rule
When acting as the orchestrator (main Claude session), **delegate to the appropriate agent** — do not do the work directly:

| Task type | `subagent_type` | `isolation` |
|-----------|-----------------|-------------|
| Code changes, bug fixes | `implementer` | `worktree` |
| Code review, PR/MR review | `reviewer` | — |
| Writing tests | `tester` | `worktree` |
| System design, exec-plans | `architect` | — |

